  "comparisonDesign": {
    "comment": [
      "**The three requirements, and the one dimension that cannot be held constant for a closed product.**",
      "",
      "**1. Run every competitor's own harness in CI and compare with ours.** **Possible for a tool we can execute** -",
      "**which is why `open-code-review` matters: it is Apache-2.0, it runs locally, and it is a direct competitor.**",
      "**Impossible for a closed SaaS**, because **there is no API through which to point Greptile at a corpus** -",
      "**its reviews exist only where Martian captured them.**",
      "",
      "**2. The judge and the embedder must be ours.** **The judge can be, for every cell**: a shipped review is text,",
      "**and text can be re-judged.** **The embedder cannot be, for a shipped review**: it was produced by their pipeline,",
      "**and re-producing it is the same impossibility as (1).**",
      "",
      "**3. Every tool must be scored on its own corpus, ours, and every other's.** **The cross-product's holes are fixed",
      "by where each tool's outputs were captured**, so **the matrix records which cells can exist rather than pretending",
      "the grid is complete.**"
    ],
    "requirements": {
      "selfRunEveryHarness": {
        "possible": "for a tool that is a program we can possess and that accepts our model",
        "runnableAndByo": [
          "agentix-e",
          "open-code-review",
          "PR-Agent (Qodo)",
          "Kodus"
        ],
        "runnableWithTheirModel": [
          "CodeRabbit CLI"
        ],
        "blocked": "a reviewer that executes server-side - **and the earlier claim that openness was the test was wrong; the test is possession and model choice**"
      },
      "ourJudgeAndEmbedder": {
        "judge": "ours for every cell - a shipped review is text and text can be re-judged",
        "embedder": "ours only where we run the tool; frozen where the tool ran itself"
      },
      "fullCrossProduct": {
        "whatIsComputable": "for each corpus, the tools whose outputs exist on it",
        "whatIsNot": "a corpus a closed tool was never pointed at"
      }
    },
    "corpora": {
      "martian-offline": {
        "size": "50 PRs \u00b7 173 golden comments",
        "license": "MIT, dataset and shipped reviews public",
        "hosts": "all 49 shipped tools, ours, and open-code-review",
        "why": "it is the only corpus where the field's own outputs are published"
      },
      "aacr-bench": {
        "size": "200 PRs \u00b7 1,505 expert-confirmed issues \u00b7 10 languages",
        "license": "Apache-2.0, goldens public, diffs fetched by URL",
        "hosts": "ours and open-code-review - the two tools that can be executed",
        "why": "its ground truth carries Repo Level issues, which are cross-file by construction"
      },
      "ours-real-ground-truth": {
        "size": "100 issues \u00b7 246 bug ranges",
        "license": "ours",
        "hosts": "ours, and any tool we can execute",
        "why": "the only corpus whose ground truth this project controls end to end"
      }
    },
    "knownHoles": [
      "a closed tool cannot be scored on aacr-bench, because no API exists to point it at that corpus",
      "a closed tool cannot be scored on our corpus, for the same reason",
      "the embedder dimension is frozen for every shipped review, and no amount of engineering changes that"
    ]
  },
  "toolRunnability": {
    "comment": [
      "**Which competitors this repository can execute - and the criterion is not whether they are open source.**",
      "",
      "**The first version of this block said `open-code-review` was runnable *because it is Apache-2.0*, which made",
      "openness look like the test.** **It is not.** **The test is two questions**: *is the reviewer a program this",
      "repository can possess and run*, and *does it accept our model*? **A closed-source CLI with a documented",
      "bring-your-own-key path passes both**, and **an open-source project whose hosted edition is the only reviewer does",
      "not.**",
      "",
      "**And the correction moves the runnable set from two entries to four**, the most important of which is",
      "**`PR-Agent`** - **Apache-2.0, CLI and Docker, and its model layer goes through LiteLLM**, which is what makes",
      "**DeepSeek a configuration line rather than a negotiation.**"
    ],
    "criterion": {
      "question1": "is the reviewer a program we can possess and run ourselves?",
      "question2": "does it accept our model (or our endpoint)?",
      "notTheCriterion": "whether the source is published - **a closed CLI with a BYO-key path is runnable, and an open project whose only reviewer is hosted is not**"
    },
    "tiers": {
      "runnableAndBringYourOwnModel": {
        "note": "**both questions answered yes** - so the judge *and* the embedder *and* the corpus are ours",
        "tools": {
          "agentix-e": {
            "how": "this repository",
            "license": "ours"
          },
          "open-code-review": {
            "how": "npm install -g @alibaba-group/open-code-review, then `ocr`",
            "license": "Apache-2.0"
          },
          "PR-Agent (Qodo)": {
            "how": "pip install / docker, CLI or GitHub Action, configured by `.pr_agent.toml`",
            "license": "Apache-2.0 (the community project Qodo donated; Qodo Merge is the hosted edition)",
            "why": "**its model layer goes through LiteLLM**, so **DeepSeek, Zhipu through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, or Ollama are configuration rather than a negotiation**",
            "evidence": "**the project also publishes its own benchmark - 60.1% F1 over 580 real-world issues** - which is a corpus we could add"
          },
          "Kodus": {
            "how": "self-hosted or Docker; documented bring-your-own-key with zero markup",
            "license": "open source",
            "why": "**BYOK across Claude, GPT, Gemini, Llama and any OpenAI-compatible endpoint**"
          }
        }
      },
      "runnableWithTheirModel": {
        "note": "**runs locally, but the model is theirs** - so the corpus is ours and the judge is ours, and **the reviewer's own model is not**",
        "tools": {
          "CodeRabbit CLI": {
            "how": "the vendor's CLI, free tier covers IDE and CLI reviews for private repositories",
            "limit": "the review model is the vendor's, so the embedder/model dimension is theirs even though the program is local"
          }
        }
      },
      "runnableStaticAnalysers": {
        "note": "**the B category is entirely local** - these are programs with no service behind them",
        "tools": [
          "Semgrep",
          "Snyk CLI",
          "CodeQL",
          "SonarQube",
          "Codacy CLI",
          "DeepSource CLI"
        ]
      },
      "runnableLocalAgents": {
        "note": "**the C category is mostly local** - and several accept any OpenAI-compatible endpoint",
        "tools": [
          "Aider",
          "Cline",
          "Continue",
          "Probe",
          "ast-grep",
          "Sourcegraph Amp"
        ]
      },
      "commerciallyUnreachable": {
        "note": "**technically runnable and not affordable** - a distinction the first version did not have",
        "tools": {
          "CodeRabbit self-hosted": "**Enterprise plan, a 500-seat minimum, on the order of $15,000 per month** - so **this is a price, not a wall**, and it belongs in a different column from the next one"
        }
      },
      "serviceOnly": {
        "note": "**there is no program to run** - the reviewer executes server-side, is reached through a GitHub App, and the model is the product",
        "reason": "Martian's fork-and-install step is the only way these outputs were ever produced, which is why their reviews are a capture on one corpus",
        "tools": [
          "Greptile",
          "cubic",
          "Augment",
          "Devin",
          "Macroscope",
          "Graphite",
          "CodeAnt",
          "Qodo (hosted)",
          "Cursor BugBot",
          "GitLab Duo",
          "Copilot review"
        ]
      }
    }
  },
  "crossProduct": {
    "comment": [
